body:
### Tested versions

tested in v4.6.3.stable.official [7d41c59c4] as well as 4.6.2 stable

### System information

Godot v4.6.3.stable - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Direct3D 12 (Mobile) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3080 Ti (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.9636) - AMD Ryzen 9 5900X 12-Core Processor (24 threads) - 31.92 GiB memory

### Issue description

A StandardMaterial3D with a normal map assigned will use the normal map correctly until `Triplanar` is enabled in the UV2 section.

When the material is converted to a generated GDShader, the following code is being added to the vertex shader even though the normal map isn't using Triplanar mapping:

```gdshader
void vertex() {
	UV = UV * uv1_scale.xy + uv1_offset.xy;

	vec3 normal = NORMAL;

	// start of problem section
	TANGENT = vec3(0.0, 0.0, -1.0) * abs(normal.x);
	TANGENT += vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0) * abs(normal.y);
	TANGENT += vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0) * abs(normal.z);
	TANGENT = normalize(TANGENT);

	BINORMAL = vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0) * abs(normal.x);
	BINORMAL += vec3(0.0, 0.0, -1.0) * abs(normal.y);
	BINORMAL += vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0) * abs(normal.z);
	BINORMAL = normalize(BINORMAL);
	// end of problem section

	// UV2 Triplanar: Enabled (with World Triplanar)
	uv2_power_normal = pow(abs(mat3(MODEL_MATRIX) * NORMAL), vec3(uv2_blend_sharpness));
	uv2_triplanar_pos = (MODEL_MATRIX * vec4(VERTEX, 1.0)).xyz * uv2_scale + uv2_offset;
	uv2_power_normal /= dot(uv2_power_normal, vec3(1.0));
	uv2_triplanar_pos *= vec3(1.0, -1.0, 1.0);
}
```

Modifying the tangents and binormals makes sense when using triplanar on UV1 since that's the UVs that the normal map uses and this code is necessary for normal maps to work with triplanar, but it doesn't make sense when enabling triplanar on UV2.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Download MRP
2. Look at the top of the reflective cube with the normal maps applied and observe that it is reflecting everything correctly
<img width="1119" height="848" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/44bda03a-2b97-4a7b-a6d1-5af0d8325613" />
3. Select the cube and set the `Triplanar` property in the `UV2` section of its material to `On`
4. Observe that the reflections on the top of the cube have changed. They are now inverted and distorted.
<img width="1118" height="849" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/15277692-57ee-41d2-9c0a-f23d81e72916" />
5. Right-click the cube's material and select `Convert to ShaderMaterial` and open the generated gdshader. Look at the vertex function.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

[triplanar-uv2-breaks-normals.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/28942452/triplanar-uv2-breaks-normals.zip)
